WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.680
Before a fat can fuel anything, it has to be unpacked in three deliberate stages.

2
00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.940
First, the triacylglycerols stored in adipose tissue are hydrolyzed by lipase,

3
00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:21.480
splitting each molecule into three free fatty acids plus a glycerol backbone, which

4
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.600
travel out into the blood toward tissues that need energy.

5
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:29.580
Second, those fatty acids are activated and shuttled across the mitochondrial

6
00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:35.920
membranes. Third, inside the matrix, the fatty acid is dismantled into acetyl-CoA

7
00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.340
units the citric acid cycle can burn.

8
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.400
Keep this roadmap in mind.

9
00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.080
Release, activation and import, then degradation.

10
00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.620
Everything that follows is just detail filling in these three moves.

11
00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:51.610
Activated fatty acids face a wall.

12
00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:57.510
Coenzyme A cannot cross the inner mitochondrial membrane, so the acyl group needs a

13
00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.330
different passport to reach the matrix.

14
00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:01.650
That passport is carnitine.

15
00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:07.350
In step one, the fatty acid is activated to acyl CoA in the cytosol.

16
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:13.430
Then carnitine palmitoyl transferase I swaps CoA for carnitine forming acyl

17
00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:17.150
carnitine, which the translocase ferries across the inner membrane.

18
00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:23.630
On the matrix side, CPT2 reverses the swap, handing the acyl group back to a fresh

19
00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:26.010
CoA so beta oxidation can begin.

20
00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:28.750
Carnitine itself is recycled back out.

21
00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:33.410
This shuttle is also the key regulatory checkpoint controlling fatty acid entry.

22
00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:38.430
The name beta-oxidation tells you exactly where the chemistry happens.

23
00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:42.550
Number the carbons starting from the carboxyl carbon as carbon 1.

24
00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:48.390
Carbon 2 is the alpha carbon, and carbon 3, the next one out, is the beta carbon.

25
00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:53.890
Beta-oxidation means we attack and oxidize that beta carbon, converting it to a

26
00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:56.090
keto group so the chain can be cleaved there.

27
00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:59.250
The delta notation is a companion convention.

28
00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:04.710
Delta N marks the first carbon of a double bond, so delta 2 means a double bond

29
00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.530
between carbons 2 and 3.

30
00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:12.090
Each round of oxidation generates acetyl-CoA, along with the reduced carriers NADH

31
00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.310
and FADH2, the energy currency we are after.

32
00:02:16.083 --> 00:02:21.063
Let's tally the payoff for fully oxidizing palmitate, a 16-carbon fat.

33
00:02:21.763 --> 00:02:26.703
Activation costs two ATP equivalents up front, so we start at minus 2.

34
00:02:27.263 --> 00:02:33.843
Seven rounds of beta-oxidation each yield 1 FADH2 and 1 NADH, and those seven

35
00:02:33.843 --> 00:02:36.123
rounds produce eight acetyl-CoA.

36
00:02:36.603 --> 00:02:41.283
Feed all eight through the citric acid cycle and run the reduced carriers through

37
00:02:41.283 --> 00:02:46.403
oxidative phosphorylation, and the books close at roughly 106 ATP.

38
00:02:47.383 --> 00:02:52.023
Compare that to glucose, which nets around 32 ATP per molecule.

39
00:02:52.663 --> 00:02:57.023
Per carbon, fat yields about 25% % more ATP than sugar.

40
00:02:57.803 --> 00:03:03.043
That density is exactly why the body stores long-term energy as fat rather than

41
00:03:03.043 --> 00:03:03.643
carbohydrate.
